course: physics
lesson: 29
title: Magnets: pulling and pushing without touching
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A magnet can pull a paper clip across the table without touching it, through an invisible region around it called its magnetic field.
explain: Every magnet has two ends, a north pole and a south pole; bring two north poles together and you feel them push apart like an invisible cushion, turn one round and they snap together.
explain: Only a few metals are pulled strongly, mainly iron, steel, nickel and cobalt, which is why a magnet grabs a steel paper clip but ignores an aluminium can.
explain: Inside a magnet, countless tiny magnets are lined up the same way; cut it in half and each half is a whole magnet with its own north and south.
explain: When electricity flows in a coil of wire, the coil becomes a magnet too, and that is the secret inside every electric motor.
fact: Magnets pull iron and steel without touching them, and the pull fades quickly with distance.
fact: Only iron, steel, nickel and cobalt are pulled strongly; most metals, such as aluminium, copper and gold, are not.
fact: Every magnet has a north pole and a south pole: like poles push apart, unlike poles pull together.
fact: Iron filings sprinkled around a magnet line up and show its invisible magnetic field; the lines bunch up at the poles, where the pull is strongest.
fact: A magnet is made of countless tiny magnets lined up together, so a magnet cut in half becomes two magnets, each with both poles.
fact: Stroking a steel paper clip or needle with a magnet lines up its tiny magnets and makes it a weak magnet; dropping or heating it jumbles them again.
fact: Magnetism passes through wood, paper, plastic and water, but a thick sheet of iron soaks most of it up.
fact: Two small strong magnets swallowed by a child can pull together through the walls of the gut and badly damage it; this is an emergency.
fact: A fridge door seal has a magnetic strip that pulls the door shut when it is close, and it can still be pushed open from inside.
fact: Maglev trains use strong magnets to float above the track and move along it; with no wheels touching, there is almost no friction.
fact: An electric motor spins because electricity in a coil of wire makes it a magnet that is pushed and pulled by other magnets.
exercise: How does a fridge magnet hold up a drawing? | It pulls on the steel door, squeezing the paper between | It glues itself to the paper | It sucks air out from behind the paper | It pulls on the paper itself
exercise: Why does a magnet hold fewer sheets of paper in a thick pile? | Its pull fades with distance | Paper is slightly magnetic and pushes back | The paper soaks up the magnetism | Thick piles are too heavy for any magnet
exercise: Which of these does a magnet pull strongly? | A steel paper clip | An aluminium drinks can | A copper coin | A gold ring
exercise: What happens when you push two north poles together? | They push each other apart | They snap together | Nothing happens at all | They both turn into south poles
exercise: What does the pattern of iron filings around a magnet show? | The invisible magnetic field around it | The electricity flowing out of its ends | The rust on the magnet | The cracks inside the magnet
exercise: Where is a magnet's pull strongest? | At its poles | Exactly in its middle | Evenly all over | Only on its painted side
exercise: What happens if you break a bar magnet in half? | You get two magnets, each with a north and a south pole | You get one north magnet and one south magnet | Both halves stop being magnets | One half keeps all the magnetism
exercise: How can you make a needle into a weak magnet? | Stroke it many times with a magnet in one direction | Leave it in the fridge overnight | Rub it hard and fast with a wool sock until it gets warm | Dip it in salty water
exercise: What can make a magnetised needle stop being a magnet? | Dropping it hard or heating it | Keeping it shut away in a dark drawer | Washing it in cold water | Wrapping it in paper
exercise: Which of these does magnetism NOT pass through easily? | A thick sheet of iron | A thick wooden table top | A sheet of paper | A glass of water
exercise: Why are swallowed magnets an emergency for children? | Two magnets can pull together through the gut and damage it | Magnets dissolve in the tummy and poison you | Magnets make your heart beat faster | Magnets pull all the iron out of your blood
exercise: Why does a fridge door shut by itself at the last moment? | A magnetic strip in the seal pulls on the steel frame | A strong spring in the hinge snaps it closed at the end | The cold air inside sucks the door shut | The light switching off pulls it closed
exercise: How can a maglev train float above its track? | Strong magnets push it up and pull it along | It rides on a cushion of steam | It is held up by long invisible wires from above | Its wheels are made of very light plastic
exercise: What is inside an electric motor that makes it spin? | A coil carrying electricity, pushed and pulled by magnets | A tiny spring that unwinds | A small engine burning petrol | A wheel pushed by the battery's chemicals
exercise: How do recycling plants sort steel cans from aluminium cans? | Big magnets pull the steel cans out | Workers weigh every can by hand | Aluminium cans float away on a strong wind | Steel cans are melted first
test: my sister says magnets stick to all metals. right? | No, mainly iron, steel, nickel and cobalt are pulled | Yes, every metal is magnetic | Yes, but only shiny metals | No, magnets only stick to plastic
test: Jake wants a magnet with only a north pole, so he cuts his magnet in half. What does he get? | Two magnets, each with north and south | One north magnet and one south one, as he hoped | Two non-magnetic bits of metal | One magnet and one bit of dust
test: How could you make a paper clip dance on top of a table without touching it? | Move a magnet underneath the table | Blow on the paper clip very gently | Rub the table with a balloon | Tap the table with a pencil
test: A toddler has swallowed two small round magnets from a toy. What should happen? | Get medical help straight away; it is an emergency | Wait, they will come out by themselves | Give her a glass of milk and forget it | Hold a big magnet to her tummy to pull them out
test: In a cartoon, a hero uses a huge magnet to pull a gold bar out of a safe. What's wrong? | Gold is not pulled by magnets | Magnets cannot work through a safe door | Nothing, gold is strongly magnetic | Magnets only work in the dark
test: Why could a child trapped inside a modern fridge still push the door open? | The door is held only by a magnet, not a lock | The fridge door is made of soft rubber | Fridges open by themselves whenever it gets warm inside | The light inside unlocks the door
test: Chloe pushes two magnets together and feels a soft springy push. What is happening? | Like poles are pushing each other apart | Unlike poles are pulling together | Air is trapped between the magnets and squashed | The magnets have run out of power
test: oi, stop stroking my sewing needle with that magnet!! what's it gonna do? | Turn the needle into a weak magnet | Melt the needle a little bit | Make the needle pull on plastic things | Nothing at all
test: Why does a maglev train hardly lose any speed to friction? | No wheels touch the track | The track is greased with oil | The train is made of feathers | It travels only downhill
test: What would happen to a toy car's motor if there were no magnets inside it? | It would not spin | It would spin twice as fast | It would spin backwards | It would turn into a magnet
test: Ella scatters iron filings round a magnet and they bunch up at the two ends. What does that tell her? | The pull is strongest at the poles | The magnet is broken in the middle | The filings are scared of the middle | The ends of the magnet are wet
exam: Identify which of the following materials is strongly attracted by a magnet. | Steel | Aluminium | Copper | Plastic
exam: Ascertain the consequence of severing a bar magnet transversely into two portions. | Each portion constitutes a complete magnet possessing both poles | One portion possesses solely a north pole and the other a south | Both portions forfeit their magnetic properties entirely | The magnetism is retained by the larger portion alone
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the interaction between two magnetic poles of like kind? | They repel one another | They attract one another | They neither attract nor repel | They attract only when separated by water
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the operation of an electric motor. | A current-carrying coil is acted upon by magnetic forces and rotates | A spring is wound and released by the battery | Fuel is combusted within a small chamber | Static charge accumulates and propels the axle
